it's great to be joined today by James  Fallows who's a national correspondent  for the Atlantic and co-author with his  Weiss wife Deborah fallows of our towns  a 100,000 mile journey into the heart of  America it's so great to talk to you  James and the book is fascinating you  went all over the country 25 cities I  believe flying to most or all of them in  a single-engine plane which you which  you piloted talk to us a little bit  about the the sort of objective of the  project the objective was essentially  just to see the US and there was a  particular predicate for it which is  that from 2006 really until 2011 dev and  I were living in China and reporting for  the Atlantic and Ed was writing a book  called dreamy and Chinese and what we've  tried to do there even while being based  within the first Shanghai couple years  then Beijing was essentially to spend  all of our time on the road and going to  every little province and on ox carts  literally in trains and buses and in  river boats so we thought we came back  to the US and back to our home in DC  it'll be worth trying to do some of the  same reporting in the US and we had for  a long time flowing around this little  propeller plane a Cirrus sr22 with a  parachute for the whole plane and we  knew there are lots of places you could  get to that way you couldn't get to very  easily by interstates or by commercial  airlines so we just thought let's see  what kind of there is there if we want  to spend time in Sioux Falls South  Dakota or in Duluth Minnesota or in  Columbus Mississippi and so this ended  up going on for more than four years and  we sort of each step we took we we learn  new things I think that it's quite  common that there are people in the US  who assuming that you're sort of in in  the income bracket where you can afford  to do some travel who will often end up  becoming more familiar with other parts  of the world than the parts of their own  country that maybe aren't as accessible  to them I know that only in recent years  have I started to go to places like I  was in rural northern Indiana a few  months ago I've been to Ohio I've been  to some other places that wouldn't sort  of be natural vacation spots did  find that or do you find that this is a  pretty common phenomena that many  traveling Americans know foreign  countries better than they know the  different corners of the US I think  that's a great point and so a homely  example of that will be that over the  last forty years now my wife and I have  lived essentially half of our time in DC  and it's always true that people who are  visiting DC go out and see museums and  restaurants and new attraction that if  you're living in the place you don't get  around the city and I'm sure that's  that's true of any big town I think it's  also the case that that again Americans  who are in the traveling class when when  you're in the u.s. it's remarkable that  these a dozen hub airports Denver  Atlanta O'Hare San Francisco Seattle etc  get something like eighty percent of all  the commercial traffic because that's  how things are concentrated in various  ways and people there air travel takes  them to places that look familiar and  when you think of exploring the world  that might be places from Tuscany to if  you're adventuresome Singapore or  Southeast Asia or Australia or maybe  Africa more than South Dakota more than  Missouri more than Mississippi and so  that was part of our wanting to to  remedy that that imbalance to one of the  things I was struck by about six eight  months ago when I went to rural northern  Indiana this was sort of my first trip  to a really red area after the election  of Donald Trump other than maybe some  parts of the Florida Panhandle was that  even though I had kind of like an on  paper understanding of how and why it is  that people in some of these parts of  the country voted so overwhelmingly for  Donald Trump I still was passing a  certain degree of judgment on it and  after spending some time living in  northern or not living but visiting  northern rural Indiana I realized that  of course they voted for Donald Trump  once I understood that in a lot of these  places the only meeting point is the  mega evangelical church as an example  how of course that's how they ended up  voting and it really shifted my  perspective about just how different the  day-to-day lives are of people in  different parts of the country do you  have any anecdotes that  that that resonates with yes and are are  complimentary you know with an e rather  than I view to that is a different kind  of explanation and puzzle before the  what the results in the 2016 election  most of the places we ended up spending  time or in states that went for Donald  Trump we've here the plane states and we  were in both Allentown and Erie  Pennsylvania and the South you know  South Carolina Georgia Mississippi  Louisiana and it was on the one hand you  would think these places would not be  expressing outrage of the kind that was  normally diagnosed after the election in  that most places the economy was slowly  improving  most places Believe It or Not were being  quite inclusive cluesive of immigrants  for example in western Kansas population  is now mainly Latino in Ben Z devices  hometown of Holland Michigan that also  is majority of Latino now there's lots  of other indications I could give you of  something resembling a what a healthy  American civic fabric and yet when it  comes to talking about national politics  there was this really polarized extreme  bitter resentment driven feeling which  was embodied among other places some of  these megachurches so you could have in  Dodge City a place that went very  heavily for Donald Trump  but they're they're City finance manager  who is very popular there is a man named  or mister de la Rosa who's there in a  Dhaka waiver and they're their school  district is majority Latino and they're  voting bond issues to keep it going so  something in this disconnect between the  increasingly tribal and resentment  driven national politics which made  people think yes Trump is against our  enemies and we really heat Hillary  Clinton because she's crooked they would  vote that way national rate and the  texture of local life was was different  from that can you talk a little bit  about are there examples that you might  have for you know our audience is mostly  a progressive audience although they're  not just in cities on the coast they're  really spread out throughout the United  dates but we've heard this theme that if  you don't understand  rural America you don't understand why  Trump won what are some of the specifics  that maybe aren't understood about rural  America so I think there's one so I  should say his background that both my  wife and I although we've you know I  worked for the Atlantic forever and  we're part of quote in the national  media unquote we are from originally  small inland towns and felt as if this  was our territory I'm from a little  place called red ones in California  excuse me which is right next to San  Bernardino it's a part of it's very  different from coast of California Deb  is from a manufacturing town in Ohio  vermilion Ohio which is west of  Cleveland so we felt as if this was  these were places familiar from our  upbringing though not our current life  one I think understandable point of  resentment is less the familiar one of  economic dislocation which of course is  real or the opioids disaster which is of  course a disaster and becoming a  nationwide one it's what I think of as  two dimensionality versus three  dimensionality which is when there are  media or movie or print magazine or  newspaper discussions of Boston or of DC  or of San Francisco or of other big  centers the tone tends to be a sort of  richly three-dimensional portrayal of  people's motives and the complexities  there and people's agency and the ways  they're trying to respond to their  environments I think there is an  awareness by most of the interior of  America of being presented two  dimensionally that media people will go  there and go to a diner and find  somebody with a trump hat and say are  you still angry used to like Trump and  just presenting people as as victims and  objects and as being significant mainly  as a sort of background players in the  national political drama and I think  that most people in any part of the US  any part of the world right whether they  like to think of themselves  as these central characters in their own  lives and they have their own  independent drama and they're making  things better and worse and they have  they have agency and they have choice  and all the rest so I think that is one  I'll give you one illustration of that  there's a piece in a leading website  that I won't name right now that was  tracing this Russian spy it was recently  arrested the young woman Russian spy  when she went out to South Dakota the  Russian spy and was sort of a lying with  the NRI there and the piece was framed  as as here we are in a lightly populated  state most familiar as the home of Mount  Rushmore this was talking about the city  of Sioux Falls which actually we spent a  long time in it's a very leading tech  city Sioux Falls they have lots of  precision agriculture that comes from  there and so I think the people in Sioux  Falls will think what the hell we're  being portrayed as kind of these bit  players in the national media caricature  of what life is like away from Brooklyn  or DC so I think that that the be an  awareness of being presented as  one-dimensional or two-dimensional  figures rather than fully fleshed out  figures is an underappreciated one I'm  curious as far as your method of  transportation your single-engine  propeller plane with a parachute  were there any at any point during this  trip situations with the plane that did  not go as planned close calls  emergencies or was that not a dramatic  part of the trip in any way we tried to  minimize the drama this was the ex into  the flying world are they no drama Obama  policy we wanted to I haven't used to my  rating I've done this for a long time  but learn to fly in good weather but but  there's always something unexpected in a  small plane so we we ended up spending  the unexpected day in Murray Kentucky  home of Murray State University when  some sparkplug suddenly failed  we're over the desert of Arizona when  suddenly all navigation info just was  blotted out in the plane the GPS system  just went entirely down we were mine  early reassured when we heard on the air  traffic control  Albuquerque senator this is United 404  we've lost all our guidance it was what  it was the Air Force was testing an  entire jamming of the GPS system upon us  they hadn't let us know about this in  advance birthday so we had surprises but  no no emergencies well the book is  fascinating it's called our towns a 100  thousand mile journey into the heart of  America the book has two authors james  Fallows who's been speaking with us as  well as his wife Deborah fallows and I  encourage you to check out the book and  James I really appreciate you talking to  me today David I'm very grateful to your  interest and that of your audience so  saying thank you

